[Unit]
Description=Recover duckbrain-http after N consecutive DARK probes (GAP-059)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# GAP-059: recovery teeth for the dark-port watchdog. duckbrain-http.service
# already has Restart=always (crash AND graceful-stop recovery), so the only
# dark state left is an explicit stop/disable/mask — which the static
# duckbrain-http-health oneshot can DETECT (exit 1) but never FIX. On
# 2026-09-19 a maintenance stop left :3000 dark for ~18 minutes until an
# operator started it by hand; this unit is the missing action.
#
# Contract: DARK (connection failure or any non-200/503 status) increments a
# consecutive-dark counter; at --confirm-probes the helper runs
# `systemctl --user start duckbrain-http.service`, records the attempt and
# re-probes /health once. ALIVE clears the counter. HUNG (exit 3 in the health
# watchdog) is NOT dark: a stuck handler is not a dead daemon, so this unit
# never restarts a daemon that is still serving traffic (OPS-002).
#
# --cooldown-s=600 is the anti-storm window: a second restart is suppressed
# for 10 minutes after an attempt, so a unit that keeps failing cannot be
# restarted once a minute forever. Intentional long downtime therefore needs
# `systemctl --user stop duckbrain-http-recover.timer` FIRST (see
# docs/guide/deployment.md § maintenance contract).
#
# Exit 1 = started but /health is still not alive (systemd may still be
# bringing it up; the next timer fire retries after the cooldown) — the
# failure is visible in `systemctl --user status` and the journal.
# /health is auth-exempt: no API keys here, ever.
ExecStart=node %h/duckbrain/scripts/watchdog-recover.js --confirm-probes=1 --cooldown-s=600
